35: GraphQL Storefront API overview
36: Copy page
37: This page is for Developer.
38: BigCommerce’s GraphQL Storefront API makes it possible to power a headless storefront; see Introduction to Headless Commerce for details. The GraphQL Storefront API also lets you query storefront data from within a Stencil theme.
39: This graph serves as a flexible interface for building shopper experiences. For example, with the GraphQL Storefront API, it is possible to do the following:
40: Query product catalog data to help shoppers find the product they’re looking for through BigCommerce’s searching, filtering, and sorting capabilities.
41: Request any product’s images at any resolution.
42: Ask for a signed-in customer’s details, such as name, email address, and attributes.
43: Look up objects, such as categories or brands, by URL, and fetch their details.
44: Create new carts, fetch existing carts, and initiate checkout.
45: GraphQL currently does not support sorting products based on pricing defined in Price Lists. Price based sorting methods with GraphQL will be based on prices on the product level.
46: Additionally, by leveraging the power of GraphQL, data for multiple resources can be returned from a single API call, which simplifies integration and increases performance so that developers can focus on building delightful shopper experiences.
47: This article is a general overview of BigCommerce’s GraphQL Storefront API; it includes sections on authentication and how to access a store’s GraphQL Playground. To see specific examples of how GraphQL can be used to query storefront data, see GraphQL Storefront API Example Queries.
48: Stores using BigCommerce’s legacy Blueprint theme framework do not support the GraphQL Storefront API or playground.
49: See it in action
50: To see a simple example of the GraphQL Storefront API in action, check out the Bootstrap + vanilla JS GraphQL Storefront API example hosted on GitHub. This example shows how a static HTML site can render dynamic product information using the GraphQL Storefront API.
51: Open the link and click submit with the sample data in the form. To see the example page with your store’s data, create a GraphQL Storefront API token against your store and paste the token into the example form. Be sure to create a token valid for this origin: https://bigcommerce.github.io.
52: Accessing the GraphQL Storefront playground
53: To access the GraphQL Storefront playground and documentation, sign in to your store and navigate to Settings > API > Storefront API Playground.
54: The GraphQL Storefront API Playground will open.
55: If the Storefront API Playground link is not visible, this may be because your store is not using a Stencil theme or any headless channels.
56: Using the GraphQL playground
57: To use the playground, input queries on the left side and then click the Play button. Query results will be displayed on the right side.
58: The following is an example query to get you started.
59: Example query
60: query MyFirstQuery {
61: site {
62: settings {
63: storeName
64: }
65: products {
66: edges {
67: node {
68: name
69: sku
70: prices {
71: retailPrice {
72: value
73: currencyCode
74: }
75: price {
76: value
77: currencyCode
78: }
79: }
80: }
81: }
82: }
83: }
84: }
85: To explore the schema, you can use the API reference, the explorer, or the playground, which lets you make live requests to your store.
86: Authentication
87: To learn more about authenticating requests to the GraphQL Storefront API, see Authenticating requests to the GraphQL Storefront API.
88: Querying within a BigCommerce storefront
89: You can make GraphQL Storefront API calls directly from within a Stencil theme or from a script in the store’s Script Manager.
90: The following example request uses the {{settings.storefront_api.token}} Handlebars object and JavaScript’s Fetch API:
91: The fetch request’s credentials property must be set to same-origin.
92: Example request: GraphQL query using Stencil auto-generated token
93: <script>
94: fetch('/graphql', {
95: method: 'POST',
96: credentials: 'same-origin',
97: headers: {
98: 'Content-Type': 'application/json',
99: 'Authorization': 'Bearer {{ settings.storefront_api.token }}' // use auto-generated token
100: },
101: body: JSON.stringify({
102: query: `query MyFirstQuery {
103: site {
104: settings {
105: storeName
106: }
107: products {
108: edges {
109: node {
110: name
111: sku
112: defaultImage {
113: url(width: 1280)
114: }
115: }
116: }
117: }
118: }
119: }`
120: })
121: })
122: .then(res => res.json())
123: .then(data => console.log(data)) // will log JSON result to browser console
124: .catch(error => console.error(error));
125: </script>
126: You can limit the number of items retrieved for the nodes that return multiple items. See the section on pagination later in this article.
127: Client libraries like Apollo offer features that can simplify GraphQL implementations, such as declarative data fetching, state management, and caching for more consistent UI components. For an example of adding Apollo Client to the Cornerstone theme, check out this Cornerstone commit on GitHub.
128: Pagination
129: The GraphQL Storefront API follows the GraphQL cursor connections specification for pagination. If this is your first time working with GraphQL pagination, see Apollo’s blog post “Explaining GraphQL connections” for an accessible introduction. If you’ve worked with other GraphQL APIs, pagination on BigCommerce should look familiar.
130: For example, consider the following query for a store’s first three products (notice first: 3 passed to products):
131: Example query: first three products
132: query paginateProducts {
133: site {
134: products (first: 3) {
135: pageInfo {
136: startCursor
137: endCursor
138: hasNextPage
139: }
140: edges {
141: cursor
142: node {
143: entityId
144: name
145: }
146: }
147: }
148: }
149: }
150: You can run this query against an example storefront using the GraphQL Storefront playground.
151: The results look something like the following:
152: Example response: first three products
153: {
154: "data": {
155: "site": {
156: "products": {
157: "pageInfo": {
158: "startCursor": "YXJyYXljb25uZWN0aW9uOjA=",
159: "endCursor": "YXJyYXljb25uZWN0aW9uOjI=",
160: "hasNextPage": true
161: },
162: "edges": [
163: {
164: "cursor": "YXJyYXljb25uZWN0aW9uOjA=",
165: "node": {
166: "entityId": 80,
167: "name": "Orbit Terrarium - Large"
168: }
169: },
170: {
171: "cursor": "YXJyYXljb25uZWN0aW9uOjE=",
172: "node": {
173: "entityId": 81,
174: "name": "Shower Curtain"
175: }
176: },
177: {
178: "cursor": "YXJyYXljb25uZWN0aW9uOjI=",
179: "node": {
180: "entityId": 82,
181: "name": "Chambray Towel"
182: }
183: }
184: ]
185: }
186: }
187: }
188: }
189: Notice the edge corresponding to entityId: 81 has a cursor of YXJyYXljb25uZWN0aW9uOjE=. We can pass that cursor to the after parameter to get the three products after entityId: 81:
190: Example query using cursor
191: query paginateProducts {
192: site {
193: products (first: 3, after: "YXJyYXljb25uZWN0aW9uOjE=") {
194: pageInfo {
195: startCursor
196: endCursor
197: hasNextPage
198: }
199: edges {
200: cursor
201: node {
202: entityId
203: name
204: }
205: }
206: }
207: }
208: }
209: The results will look something like this (notice the last product entityId: 82 is now the first product):
210: Example response using cursor
211: {
212: "data": {
213: "site": {
214: "products": {
215: "pageInfo": {
216: "startCursor": "YXJyYXljb25uZWN0aW9uOjI=",
217: "endCursor": "YXJyYXljb25uZWN0aW9uOjQ=",
218: "hasNextPage": true
219: },
220: "edges": [
221: {
222: "cursor": "YXJyYXljb25uZWN0aW9uOjI=",
223: "node": {
224: "entityId": 82,
225: "name": "Chambray Towel"
226: }
227: },
228: {
229: "cursor": "YXJyYXljb25uZWN0aW9uOjM=",
230: "node": {
231: "entityId": 83,
232: "name": "Hand & Body Cream"
233: }
234: },
235: {
236: "cursor": "YXJyYXljb25uZWN0aW9uOjQ=",
237: "node": {
238: "entityId": 84,
239: "name": "Room Spray"
240: }
241: }
242: ]
243: }
244: }
245: }
246: }
247: You can use the same approach to slice any GraphQL connection and paginate through the slices using startCursor and endCursor. For example, the following query gets the first thirty brands:
248: Example query using slices
249: query brands {
250: site {
251: brands (first: 30) {
252: pageInfo {
253: startCursor
254: endCursor
255: }
256: edges {
257: cursor
258: node {
259: name
260: }
261: }
262: }
263: }
264: }
265: And given the following results:
266: Example response using slices
267: {
268: "data": {
269: "site": {
270: "brands": {
271: "pageInfo": {
272: "startCursor": "YXJyYXljb25uZWN0aW9uOjA=",
273: "endCursor": "YXJyYXljb25uZWN0aW9uOjM="
274: },
275: "edges": [
276: {
277: "cursor": "YXJyYXljb25uZWN0aW9uOjA=",
278: "node": {
279: "name": "Sagaform"
280: }
281: },
282: ...
283: ]
284: }
285: }
286: }
287: }
288: You can retrieve the next thirty by making a new query and passing in the endCursor from the first page of results:
289: Example query using slices and cursor
290: query brands {
291: site {
292: brands (first: 30, after:"YXJyYXljb25uZWN0aW9uOjM=") {
293: pageInfo {
294: startCursor
295: endCursor
296: }
297: edges {
298: cursor
299: node {
300: name
301: }
302: }
303: }
304: }
305: }
306: If first is not specified, a query returns only 10 items by default.
307: GraphQL bi-directional pagination
308: The GraphQL Storefront API supports bi-directional pagination, enabling both forward and backward navigation through data. The following pagination options are available:
309: Base query: Fetching the initial set of data.
310: Forward pagination: Moving forward in the dataset.
311: Backward pagination: Moving backward through the dataset.
312: Base Query:
313: The base query provides the first page of results. You can use this query as the starting point for the next pagination queries. The example below demonstrates how to query the first 50 products.
314: Example base query
315: query {
316: site {
317: products(first: 50) {
318: pageInfo {
319: startCursor
320: endCursor
321: hasNextPage
322: hasPreviousPage
323: }
324: edges {
325: cursor
326: node {
327: entityId
328: name
329: }
330: }
331: }
332: }
333: }
334: In this query:
335: first: 50: Retrieves the first 50 products.
336: pageInfo: Contains pagination metadata:
337: startCursor: The cursor for the first product.
338: endCursor: The cursor for the last product.
339: hasNextPage: Indicates if there are more products after the current page.
340: hasPreviousPage: Indicates if there are products before the current page.
341: Forward pagination:
342: For forward pagination, you can use the hasNextPage=true and hasPreviousPage=false flags. This allows you to load the next set of results. The example below queries the first 5 products.
343: Example of forward pagination
344: query {
345: site {
346: products(first: 5) {
347: pageInfo {
348: startCursor
349: endCursor
350: hasNextPage
351: hasPreviousPage
352: }
353: edges {
354: cursor
355: node {
356: entityId
357: name
358: }
359: }
360: }
361: }
362: }
363: In this query:
364: first: 5: Retrieves the first 5 products.
365: The pageInfo object provides information about whether there are additional pages to fetch in the forward direction (hasNextPage).
366: Backward pagination:
367: To paginate backwards, you can use the hasNextPage=false and hasPreviousPage=true flags. The flags are helpful for retrieving data from previous pages. The example below shows how to query the last 5 products.
368: Example of forward pagination
369: query {
370: site {
371: products(last: 5, before: "YXJyYXljb25uZWN0aW9uOjU=") {
372: pageInfo {
373: startCursor
374: endCursor
375: hasNextPage
376: hasPreviousPage
377: }
378: edges {
379: cursor
380: node {
381: entityId
382: name
383: }
384: }
385: }
386: }
387: }
388: In this query:
389: last: 5: Fetches the first 5 products.
390: before: "YXJyYXljb25uZWN0aW9uOjU=": A cursor from a previous result, indicating where the backward pagination should begin.
391: The pageInfo will tell you if there are more products in the backward direction (via hasPreviousPage) and if there are pages ahead (via hasNextPage).
392: Complexity limits
393: Complexity scores estimate the load that a single GraphQL request may generate. BigCommerce calculates the complexity score using an algorithm before executing the call, based on the provided query.
394: The API sets a complexity limit of 10,000 for each request to prevent excessive loads. Because all work is generated per request, the limit is fixed per request, regardless of the number of requests sent.
395: When you send a valid request to the GraphQL Storefront API, the API returns the query complexity as an integer using the HTTP response header X-Bc-Graphql-Complexity.
396: If a query’s complexity score exceeds the complexity limit, an error response similar to the following appears:
397: Example response with complexity error
398: {
399: "errors": [
400: {
401: "message": "The query is too complex as it has a complexity score of 12230 out of 10000. Please remove some elements and try again"
402: }
403: ]
404: }
405: The complexity limit error is usually caused by queries with a large quantity of deeply nested objects; for example, this query requests four different product collections together with their prices, variants, options, and option values.
406: Example query with excessive complexity
407: query {
408: site {
409: products(first: 50) {
410: edges {
411: node {
412: ...ProductFields
413: }
414: }
415: }
416: featuredProducts(first:50) {
417: edges {
418: node {
419: ...ProductFields
420: }
421: }
422: }
423: newestProducts(first:50) {
424: edges {
425: node {
426: ...ProductFields
427: }
428: }
429: }
430: bestSellingProducts(first:50) {
431: edges {
432: node {
433: ...ProductFields
434: }
435: }
436: }
437: }
438: }
439: fragment ProductFields on Product {
440: name
441: prices {
442: price {
443: value
444: currencyCode
445: }
446: retailPrice {
447: value
448: currencyCode
449: }
450: }
451: variants(first: 250) {
452: edges {
453: node {
454: entityId
455: depth {
456: value
457: unit
458: }
459: sku
460: }
461: }
462: }
463: productOptions(first: 50) {
464: edges {
465: node {
466: displayName
467: ... on MultipleChoiceOption {
468: values(first: 50) {
469: edges {
470: node {
471: label
472: entityId
473: }
474: }
475: }
476: }
477: }
478: }
479: }
480: }
481: You can reduce the complexity of this query by changing the number of products queried in each collection from first:50 to first:10:
482: Example query with limited complexity
483: query {
484: site {
485: products(first:10) { // <--- reducing quantity requested reduces complexity score
486: // ...
487: }
488: }
489: }
490: Filter arguments and character limits don’t contribute to complexity.
491: Example query
492: query {
493: site {
494: content {
495: blog {
496: posts (filters: {tags:["Most Popular"]}) { // Filter argument
497: edges {
498: node {
499: plainTextSummary (characterLimit: 100) // Character limit
500: }
501: }
502: }
503: }
504: }
505: }
506: }
507: To reduce complexity, reduce the number of objects requested. For example, you can do the following:
508: Limit collections to a smaller page size (for example, first:10 instead of first:50).
509: Reduce the number of items in nested collections, which exponentially affects complexity.
510: Request fewer fields on objects, and don’t request fields you don’t need.
511: Mutations have a higher complexity than queries; you can only send one mutation in a single request. Pagination info also contributes to complexity.
512: Query depth
513: In addition to the complexity limit, there is a query depth limit. The limit is 16. Queries must not exceed the query depth limit and the complexity limit.
514: Every parent type adds an additional level of depth, as shown in the following examples.
515: Example query with depth of 11
516: query { // depth 0
517: site { // depth 1
518: products { // depth 2
519: edges { // depth 3
520: node { // depth 4
521: entityId // depth 5
522: variants { // depth 5
523: edges { // depth 6
524: node { // depth 7
525: entityId // depth 8
526: options { // depth 8
527: edges { // depth 9
528: node { // depth 10
529: entityId // depth 11
530: }
531: }
532: }
533: }
534: }
535: }
536: }
537: }
538: }
539: }
540: }
541: Example query with depth of six
542: query { // depth 0
543: site { // depth 1
544: categoryTree { // depth 2
545: ...CategoryFields // depth 3
546: children { // depth 3
547: ...CategoryFields // depth 4
548: children { // depth 4
549: ...CategoryFields // depth 5
550: }
551: }
552: }
553: }
554: }
555: fragment CategoryFields on CategoryTreeItem {
556: name
557: }
